Topic: Mobilising knowledge across disciplinary lines The Aim of the Task: We live, learn and work in a world that is undergoing rapid and profound transformation. Advances in digital technologies and artificial intelligence, increasing flexibility in learning and working arrangements, continuously evolving professional roles and opportunities, blurring of boundaries between work and life, and rising awareness of the importance and impact of professional satisfaction, sense of belonging and wellbeing are reshaping roles, capability demands, and how people experience learning and work (Beier et al., 2025; Cascio, 2019; Osher & Pittman, 2024; Parker & Knight, 2023). Developing our capacity for continuous learning across our life course is now more essential than ever to ensure workforce participation and professional growth (Beier, et al., 2025; Lund et al., 2021). Drawing on interdisciplinary research, the Science of learning has evidenced that meaningful learning is context-dependent, weaving together knowledge, skill, cognition, emotion, social interaction, lived experience, culture and context (Immordino-Yang, et al., 2024 ). Learning also develops within a learning ecosystem – recognising the reciprocal influence of individual, interpersonal, social, cultural, political, geographical, historical factors and influences. In ‘ Weaving a Colorful Cloth’, Immordino-Yang and colleagues (2024) describe how learning is subjective, shaped by interdependent factors, which they articulate through five interrelated principles. Using the metaphor of weaving, the authors illustrate how, when we learn, multiple ‘threads’ weave together to create a unique autobiographical experience. Autobiographical, meaningful learning involves the active process of “ internally focused sense-making and externally focused self-expression and action in the world ” (Immordino-Yang et al., 2024, p. 11). The Task: In this task, you are to examine your own meaningful learning and how you can foster the capabilities needed for continuous, agile learning. Through deep reflection and critical engagement with the literature and learning theory you will examine your autobiographical learning ecosystem: what has shaped and is shaping you as a learner, what beliefs, values, knowledge, skills, experiences, emotions and relationships support and nurture you as a learner. Through this reflection and exploration of the literature you will create a portfolio to illustrate how the threads of your unique learning ecosystem contribute to weaving your own ‘colourful cloth’. Read and reflect on the core reading in relation to yourself as a learner, referencing the key principles in your task (we will also be engaging in multiple activities related to this reading across the semester): Immordino-Yang, M. H., Nasir, N. S., Cantor, P., & Yoshikawa, H. (2024). Weaving a colorful cloth: Centering education on humans’ emergent developmental potentials. Review of Research in Education, 47(1), 1-45. https://doi.org/10.3102/0091732X231223516 (Original work published 2023) Each week ( Weeks 1-9), reflect on the course content, readings, discussions and activities and consider how these ideas inform your understanding of yourself as a continuous learner and how you can use these insights to enhance meaningful learning. a. What insights challenge/ affirm/ extend your thinking about meaningful learning? b. What do you think has contributed to your ‘colourful cloth’ as a learner? Give examples of your own learning experiences. c. What questions or wonderings do these insights raise for you? d. Illustrate your thinking, idea generation, reflection, problem solving 3. Draw on the course readings and additional literature to support your reflections and examination of meaningful learning. 4. Synthesise your reflections across Weeks 1-9 in a brief summary (approx. 500 words) of what meaningful learning is for you, illustrating the components that are woven together to create your unique colourful cloth. Read full task description Hide full task description
